\title{\vspace{-1.2cm}\textbf{When Does Density Beat Centroids?\\
A Comparative Study of K-Means and DBSCAN-Family Clustering\\
for Spatial Hotspot Detection}}
\author{Data Mining Course Project}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\maketitle
\vspace{-0.9cm}

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
We compare four clustering algorithms --- K-Means with random initialisation,
K-Means++, DBSCAN and HDBSCAN --- on three datasets drawn from two application
settings: spatial hotspot detection, where no ground truth exists, and protein
localisation, where it does. Our motivating application is the discovery of
spatially coherent activity zones from point events, evaluated on 23{,}412
global earthquake epicentres and 6{,}000 New York City ride-hailing pickups. A
third dataset, the 336-protein \emph{Ecoli} localisation benchmark, supplies the
ground-truth labels the spatial data lacks. Three findings emerge. First, the
algorithms disagree not marginally but categorically: on the earthquake data
K-Means partitions the globe into three convex wedges that cut across tectonic
boundaries, while DBSCAN recovers 68 arc-shaped boundary segments while
discarding only 2\% of events as noise. Second, and more consequentially, on the
labelled dataset the silhouette coefficient ranks the four methods in almost
exactly the reverse order of the Adjusted Rand Index (rank correlation
$-0.80$): the method with the best silhouette, HDBSCAN, has the worst ARI, and
the method with the worst silhouette, K-Means++, has the best ARI. Since
internal indices are the only selection tool available on unlabelled spatial
data, this result directly undermines the standard practice of choosing
clustering parameters by maximising silhouette. Third, a dimensionality ablation
reveals a clean crossover: DBSCAN outperforms K-Means++ at two and three
dimensions (ARI 0.575 and 0.618 versus 0.468 and 0.466) and loses decisively at
seven (0.455 versus 0.731). We conclude that algorithm choice is governed by
cluster geometry and intrinsic dimensionality rather than by any internal
quality score, and we give an explicit selection protocol that makes the
operational requirements of the application, not the metric, the binding
constraint.
\end{abstract}

% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Motivation}
Many operational decisions reduce to the same question: given a cloud of
recorded events, where are the concentrations, how many are there, and which
events belong to no concentration at all? A seismologist asks it of earthquake
epicentres in order to delineate active fault systems. A fleet operator asks it
of ride pickups in order to decide where to position idle vehicles. In neither
case is the number of groups known in advance, in neither case are the groups
convex, and in both cases a substantial fraction of events are genuinely
isolated and should not be forced into any group.

These three properties --- unknown cluster count, arbitrary shape, and the
existence of true noise --- are precisely the assumptions that separate
centroid-based clustering from density-based clustering. K-Means requires the
analyst to supply $k$, implicitly models every cluster as an isotropic ball, and
assigns every point to some cluster. DBSCAN requires neither $k$ nor convexity
and labels low-density points as noise. The application therefore does not merely
provide a testbed for comparing the two families; it predicts which should win.
Our experiments test that prediction.

\subsection{Problem statement}
A comparison of this kind faces an awkward obstacle. The datasets where the
question is operationally interesting are exactly the datasets where no ground
truth exists --- nobody has labelled which earthquake belongs to which fault
segment. Evaluation must therefore fall back on internal validity indices such
as the silhouette coefficient. But the silhouette is defined through
within-cluster and between-cluster distances to \emph{all} other points of a
cluster, a formulation that rewards compact, well-separated, roughly spherical
groups. It therefore encodes the assumption made by one of the two algorithm
families under comparison. Using it as the arbiter is not a neutral act.

This paper takes that tension as its subject. We ask not only which algorithm
recovers better structure, but whether the metrics available in the unlabelled
setting would have told us so.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}\itemsep1pt
\item A unified comparison of four clustering algorithms across three datasets
spanning two application domains, with a single evaluation harness and an
explicit, application-driven parameter-selection protocol
(Section~\ref{sec:setup}).
\item Quantification of a systematic disagreement between internal and external
validity indices on labelled data (rank correlation $-0.80$), and a
demonstration that the same mechanism corrupts parameter selection on unlabelled
spatial data (Sections~\ref{sec:disagree} and \ref{sec:operating}).
\item Four ablations isolating initialisation, distance metric, feature scaling
and intrinsic dimensionality, including a crossover point at which the ranking
of the two algorithm families reverses (Section~\ref{sec:ablation}).
\item A practical selection rule derived from the failure modes observed, rather
than from metric values (Section~\ref{sec:guidance}).
\end{itemize}

% ============================================================
\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Centroid-based clustering.} Lloyd's algorithm for $k$-means minimises
within-cluster sum of squares by alternating assignment and centroid update; it
converges to a local optimum whose quality depends heavily on initialisation.
Arthur and Vassilvitskii's k-means++ seeding selects initial centres with
probability proportional to squared distance from the nearest existing centre,
achieving an $O(\log k)$ expected approximation to the optimal objective. We
treat the two as an initialisation ablation rather than as separate algorithms,
since they share an identical update rule.

\textbf{Density-based clustering.} DBSCAN (Ester et al., 1996) defines clusters
as maximal sets of density-connected points, where a point is a core point if at
least \texttt{minPts} points lie within $\varepsilon$. Points reachable from no
core point are labelled noise. The method needs no $k$ and imposes no shape
constraint, but the single global $\varepsilon$ assumes uniform density across
the dataset. OPTICS relaxes this by producing a reachability ordering rather than
a flat partition; HDBSCAN (Campello et al., 2013) goes further, building a
hierarchy over the mutual-reachability distance and extracting a flat clustering
by maximising cluster stability, which removes $\varepsilon$ entirely and leaves
only a minimum cluster size.

\textbf{Validity indices.} Internal indices --- silhouette, Davies--Bouldin,
Calinski--Harabasz --- score a partition using only the data and the labels.
External indices --- Adjusted Rand Index, Normalised Mutual Information ---
compare against ground truth. It is known in principle that internal indices
embed geometric assumptions; our contribution is to measure how large the
resulting distortion is in a setting where only internal indices are available.

% ============================================================
\section{Methods}

\subsection{K-Means and K-Means++}
K-Means seeks centroids $\{\mu_1,\dots,\mu_k\}$ minimising
$J=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\min_{j}\lVert x_i-\mu_j\rVert^2$. Each iteration assigns every
point to its nearest centroid and recomputes centroids as cluster means, giving
$O(nkdi)$ time for $i$ iterations. The objective is exactly the
sum of squared Euclidean distances, so the implied cluster model is an isotropic
Gaussian of equal variance; non-convex or elongated structure cannot be
represented. Every point receives a label, so outliers displace centroids rather
than being isolated. K-Means++ modifies only the initial centre selection.

\subsection{DBSCAN}
DBSCAN takes $\varepsilon$ and \texttt{minPts}. A point is a \emph{core point}
if $|N_\varepsilon(p)|\geq\texttt{minPts}$; clusters are the transitive closure
of direct density-reachability among core points, extended to border points; all
remaining points are noise. Complexity is $O(n\log n)$ with a spatial index.
The method makes no shape assumption and requires no $k$, but a single global
$\varepsilon$ presumes comparable density everywhere --- the assumption that
fails when a sparse region and a dense region must both be resolved.
Critically for our application, $\varepsilon$ carries physical units: on spatial
data it is a distance in kilometres, and is therefore directly interpretable,
whereas $k$ is not.

\subsection{HDBSCAN}
HDBSCAN replaces the global $\varepsilon$ with the \emph{mutual reachability
distance}
\[
d_{\text{mreach}}(a,b)=\max\bigl\{\text{core}_k(a),\ \text{core}_k(b),\ d(a,b)\bigr\},
\]
builds a minimum spanning tree under this metric, condenses the resulting
hierarchy using a minimum cluster size, and selects the set of clusters
maximising total stability. The only required parameter is
\texttt{min\_cluster\_size}. Because different branches of the hierarchy are cut
at different density levels, clusters of differing density can coexist --- the
precise failure mode of DBSCAN.

\begin{table}[t]\centering\small
\caption{Structural assumptions of the four methods.}
\label{tab:assump}
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
& \textbf{K-Means} & \textbf{K-Means++} & \textbf{DBSCAN} & \textbf{HDBSCAN} \\
\midrule
Cluster shape assumed      & isotropic & isotropic & arbitrary & arbitrary \\
Requires $k$               & yes & yes & no & no \\
Explicit noise label       & no & no & yes & yes \\
Handles varying density    & no & no & no & yes \\
Parameter is interpretable & no & no & yes ($\varepsilon$ in km) & partly \\
Deterministic              & no & no & yes & yes \\
Time complexity            & $O(nkdi)$ & $O(nkdi)$ & $O(n\log n)$ & $O(n\log n)$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

% ============================================================
\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Datasets}
Table~\ref{tab:data} summarises the three datasets; Figure~\ref{fig:data} shows
them. Two are spatial and unlabelled, reflecting the application; the third is
non-spatial and labelled, and exists to test whether the conclusions drawn from
internal metrics on the first two can be trusted.

\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
\caption{Datasets. The first two carry no ground truth, which is precisely the
condition the application imposes.}
\label{tab:data}
\begin{tabular}{llrrcl}
\toprule
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{$n$} & \textbf{$d$} & \textbf{Classes} & \textbf{Application question} \\
\midrule
D1 & Earthquakes & 23{,}412 & 2 & --- & Which tectonic boundary segments are active? \\
D2 & UberNYC     & 6{,}000  & 2 & --- & Where should idle vehicles be positioned? \\
D3 & Ecoli       & 336      & 7 & 8   & Do sequence signals predict localisation site? \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\textbf{D1 --- Earthquakes.} Global epicentres of magnitude $\geq5.5$ recorded
between 1965 and 2016. Features are latitude and longitude. Seismicity
concentrates along plate boundaries, which form long, curved, branching arcs of
strongly varying width and density, with genuinely isolated intraplate events
scattered between them. This is a maximally unfavourable geometry for a
centroid-based method.

\textbf{D2 --- UberNYC.} A random sample of 6{,}000 ride-hailing pickups in New
York City during April 2014, restricted to a bounding box that removes GPS
artefacts. Demand concentrates along the Manhattan street grid and at airports,
producing clusters that differ in density by more than an order of magnitude
between Midtown and the outer boroughs.

\textbf{D3 --- Ecoli.} 336 proteins described by seven signal-sequence scores
computed from the amino acid sequence, labelled with one of eight subcellular
localisation sites. The class distribution is severely imbalanced, with several
classes containing fewer than five members --- a structure that lets us ask
whether a method's noise label discards signal.

\subsection{Preprocessing and coordinate handling}
Geographic coordinates in degrees are not a metric space: one degree of
longitude spans 111\,km at the equator and 71\,km at $50^\circ$ latitude.
We therefore project both spatial datasets to local kilometres using an
equirectangular projection about the data mean latitude, so that $\varepsilon$
and the silhouette are expressed in kilometres and the centroid- and
density-based methods are compared on the same distance scale. Section
\ref{sec:metric} ablates this choice against raw-degree Euclidean distance and
against exact haversine distance. Ecoli features are standardised to zero mean
and unit variance; Section~\ref{sec:scaling} ablates this.

\subsection{Evaluation metrics}
We report \textbf{internal} indices --- silhouette ($\uparrow$),
Davies--Bouldin ($\downarrow$), Calinski--Harabasz ($\uparrow$) --- computed with
noise points excluded, since a noise point belongs to no cluster and its
inclusion would penalise density methods arbitrarily. Where ground truth exists
we additionally report \textbf{external} indices --- ARI and NMI ($\uparrow$) ---
computed with noise retained as its own label, so that discarding points is
penalised. Silhouette on D1 is estimated from a random subsample of 5{,}000
points for tractability. We also report the number of clusters, the noise
fraction, and wall-clock runtime.

\subsection{Parameter selection protocol}\label{sec:protocol}
All four methods are tuned under one protocol, so that no method is advantaged.

\emph{With ground truth} (D3): the parameter maximising ARI is selected. This is
an oracle protocol and reports each method's best achievable performance.

\emph{Without ground truth} (D1, D2): only internal indices are available. An
unconstrained silhouette search, however, collapses to a degenerate solution ---
on both spatial datasets the silhouette is maximised at $k=2$, which answers no
operational question. We therefore impose the requirements the application
itself dictates: a solution must return between 3 and 150 clusters and may
discard at most 40\% of events as noise. Within that feasible region we maximise
silhouette. This constraint is applied identically to K-Means, DBSCAN and
HDBSCAN.

$\varepsilon$ candidates are generated from the k-distance curve: the distance to
the \texttt{minPts}-th nearest neighbour is computed for every point, sorted, and
the knee located as the point of maximum deviation from the chord joining the
curve's endpoints (Figure~\ref{fig:kdist}). The grid spans $0.10\times$ to
$3.0\times$ the knee value, crossed with
$\texttt{minPts}\in\{4,8,16,32\}$ (D1, D2) or $\{3,5,8,12\}$ (D3).
For K-Means, $k$ ranges over $2\ldots25$; for HDBSCAN,
\texttt{min\_cluster\_size} over a geometric grid.

\subsection{Reproducibility}
All experiments use scikit-learn 1.8.0 on a single CPU core. Initialisation
ablations average over 30 seeds and report mean $\pm$ standard deviation; all
other runs use seed 42. Every number in this report is produced by
\texttt{src/run.py} and written to \texttt{results/*.csv}; every figure by
\texttt{src/figures.py}.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f1_datasets.png}
\caption{The three datasets. D1 and D2 are shown in geographic coordinates; D3
is projected onto its first two principal components (53\% of variance). The
arc-shaped, branching structure of D1 and the extreme density contrast of D2 are
both visible without any clustering.}
\label{fig:data}
\end{figure}

