\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f2_qualitative.png}
\caption{Qualitative comparison. Rows are datasets, columns are methods; grey
points are noise. The D1 row is the clearest illustration in the study: K-Means
partitions the globe into three convex wedges that slice across the
Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the Pacific Ring of Fire, while DBSCAN and HDBSCAN trace
the boundary arcs themselves.}
\label{fig:qual}
\end{figure}

Three observations follow directly.

\textbf{The spatial results are structurally, not marginally, different.}
On D1, K-Means at its selected $k=3$ produces three longitude wedges. These are
compact and well separated in the Euclidean sense --- silhouette 0.654 --- and
completely uninformative seismologically, because a cluster boundary drawn
through the middle of the Pacific plate corresponds to no physical structure.
DBSCAN at the same operating point returns 48 clusters that follow the plate
boundaries, at a silhouette of 0.401. The method that scores worse on the
internal index is the one that answers the question.

\textbf{Internal indices do not agree with each other either.} On D1, silhouette
selects HDBSCAN, Calinski--Harabasz selects DBSCAN, and Davies--Bouldin selects
HDBSCAN. On D2, silhouette selects DBSCAN while Calinski--Harabasz selects
K-Means --- by a factor of nearly four. An analyst choosing a method by internal
index alone would reach a different conclusion depending on which index was
consulted.

\textbf{Initialisation matters more than expected on D3.} K-Means and K-Means++
run at identical $k$ with identical update rules differ by 0.24 ARI (0.493
versus 0.731). This is the single largest effect of any parameter change in the
study, and it arises purely from seeding. Section~\ref{sec:init} quantifies it.

\subsection{Parameter sensitivity}
Figure~\ref{fig:ksweep} shows the $k$ sweep. On both spatial datasets the
silhouette curve is monotonically decreasing from $k=2$, so the unconstrained
optimum is the smallest admissible $k$ --- the degeneracy that motivated the
constrained protocol of Section~\ref{sec:protocol}. The inertia elbow, by
contrast, falls at $k=8$ (D1) and $k=7$ (D2), which is both more plausible and
more useful. On D3, where ARI is available, the ARI curve peaks at $k=7$ against
a true class count of 8; silhouette peaks at $k=5$. Even here, where the data
are relatively well behaved, silhouette underestimates the number of groups.

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f3_ksweep.png}
\caption{K-Means $k$ sweep. Dashed line: inertia elbow. Dotted line: $k$
selected under the constrained protocol. On D1 and D2 the silhouette declines
monotonically in $k$, so the constraint --- not the metric --- determines the
answer.}
\label{fig:ksweep}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f4_kdistance.png}
\caption{k-distance curves with the detected knee. The knee gives
$\varepsilon=274$\,km for D1 and $0.69$\,km for D2 --- both physically
meaningful quantities, unlike $k$.}
\label{fig:kdist}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:dbgrid} shows the DBSCAN grid. The dominant pattern is that
cluster count and noise fraction are far more sensitive to $\varepsilon$ than to
\texttt{minPts}: across the grid, varying $\varepsilon$ by a factor of 30 moves
the cluster count over two orders of magnitude, while varying \texttt{minPts}
from 4 to 32 changes it by less than a factor of three. The practical
implication is that effort spent tuning \texttt{minPts} is largely wasted;
$\varepsilon$ is the parameter that matters.

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f5_dbscan_grid.png}
\caption{DBSCAN parameter grid: cluster count (top, log scale) and noise
fraction (bottom). $\varepsilon$ dominates; \texttt{minPts} is a secondary
control.}
\label{fig:dbgrid}
\end{figure}

\subsection{The operating-point problem}\label{sec:operating}
Figure~\ref{fig:op} isolates the practical consequence. On D1 the k-distance
knee gives $\varepsilon=274$\,km, which yields 68 clusters that trace the plate
boundaries almost perfectly while discarding only 2\% of events --- and scores a
silhouette of $-0.03$, essentially the worst possible value. The
silhouette-optimal setting, $\varepsilon=123$\,km, raises the score to 0.40 by
fragmenting the boundaries and discarding 29\% of the events. On D2 the same
pattern appears in reverse: the knee at $\varepsilon=0.69$\,km separates
Manhattan, Brooklyn, JFK and LaGuardia into 11 usable zones; the
silhouette-optimal $\varepsilon=2.07$\,km merges them into three, collapsing all
of Manhattan into a single undifferentiated blob that provides no basis for
positioning vehicles.

In both cases the silhouette prefers the operationally useless answer. It does
so for a coherent reason: elongated and irregular clusters have large
within-cluster distances by construction, so the index penalises exactly the
structure the application is trying to find.

\begin{figure}[t]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f10_operating_point.png}
\caption{Operating-point selection. Left: K-Means at the inertia elbow. Centre:
DBSCAN at the k-distance knee. Right: DBSCAN at the silhouette optimum. On D1
the silhouette prefers a solution that discards 29\% of events over one that
discards 2\% and recovers the plate boundaries; on D2 it prefers three merged
blobs over eleven usable demand zones.}
\label{fig:op}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Internal and external indices disagree systematically}\label{sec:disagree}
D3 lets us test the above claim against ground truth, and the result is stark.
Ranking the four methods by silhouette gives HDBSCAN $>$ DBSCAN $>$ K-Means++ $>$
K-Means. Ranking them by ARI gives almost exactly the reverse: K-Means++ $>$
K-Means $>$ DBSCAN $>$ HDBSCAN. The Pearson correlation between the two rank
vectors is $\mathbf{-0.80}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:disagree}). The best method by
silhouette is the worst by ARI, and vice versa.

The mechanism is visible in the noise column of Table~\ref{tab:main}. HDBSCAN
discards 37\% of the proteins. Removing the sparse, ambiguous points inflates
the silhouette of what remains, but those discarded points include entire rare
localisation classes, which is why ARI collapses to 0.339. The index rewards the
method for throwing away the hardest and most biologically interesting part of
the problem.

This is the study's central result, because it transfers. On D1 and D2 we cannot
compute ARI --- but there is no reason to expect the distortion to be smaller
there. If anything it should be larger, since the spatial clusters are far less
convex than the Ecoli classes. Conclusions drawn from silhouette on unlabelled
spatial data should therefore be treated as unreliable by default.

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.72\textwidth]{figs/f8_metric_disagreement.png}
\caption{Ecoli: internal versus external validity. Left: raw values. Right:
rank agreement; points on the dashed line would indicate agreement. The
observed rank correlation is $-0.80$.}
\label{fig:disagree}
\end{figure}

% ============================================================
\section{Ablation Studies}\label{sec:ablation}

\subsection{A: Initialisation}\label{sec:init}
K-Means and K-Means++ differ only in seeding. Over 30 seeds at the selected $k$
(Table~\ref{tab:init}, Figure~\ref{fig:init}), K-Means++ reduces the mean
objective by 8.4\% on D1 and by 25.2\% on D3, and reduces the iteration count on
all three datasets --- most sharply on D3, from 17.7 to 11.0. On D2 the mean
objective is statistically indistinguishable and only the iteration count
improves.

The variance result is more nuanced than the standard account suggests. On D1
K-Means++ reduces the coefficient of variation from 11.0\% to 9.1\%, but on D3
it \emph{increases} it from 8.4\% to 12.9\% --- while simultaneously improving
the mean by a quarter. K-Means++ is therefore not simply a variance-reduction
technique here: on D3 it moves the whole distribution to a better region of the
objective landscape and explores that region more widely. The practical
recommendation is unchanged (use K-Means++), but the reason is the mean, not the
spread.

