\subsection{B: Distance metric on geographic data}\label{sec:metric}
We compare three treatments of geographic coordinates at matched $\varepsilon$:
exact haversine distance, naive Euclidean distance on raw degrees, and Euclidean
distance on the projected kilometre coordinates used throughout.

On D1 all three return 48 clusters, but haversine retains more data (26.4\% noise
versus 29.3\%) --- the projection understates distances at high latitude, so
marginally fewer points qualify as core points. On D2 the discrepancy is larger
in relative terms: raw-degree Euclidean returns 6 clusters where haversine and
projected-km return 3, because a degree of longitude at $40.7^\circ$N is only
0.76 of a degree of latitude, so a circular $\varepsilon$ ball in degree space is
an ellipse on the ground, elongated east--west. Haversine costs roughly
$2\times$ the runtime of the projected version (0.40\,s versus 0.13\,s on D1).

The conclusion is that raw-degree Euclidean should not be used, but that a local
projection is an adequate and cheaper substitute for haversine at city scale;
at global scale haversine is preferable if the noise fraction matters.

\subsection{C: Feature scaling}\label{sec:scaling}
On D3, standardisation raises K-Means++ ARI from 0.497 to 0.731, a 47\% relative
improvement. DBSCAN, given its own best $\varepsilon$ in each condition, moves in
the opposite direction: 0.481 to 0.455.

The asymmetry is informative. K-Means minimises total squared distance, so a
feature with larger numeric range dominates the objective and the recovered
partition; equalising the ranges is therefore a direct improvement. DBSCAN only
asks whether a neighbourhood contains \texttt{minPts} points, and since
$\varepsilon$ is re-tuned per condition, a global rescaling is largely absorbed
by the re-tuning. Standardisation is close to mandatory for centroid methods and
close to irrelevant for density methods when $\varepsilon$ is tuned --- though
note this holds for \emph{uniform} rescaling; it would not hold if only some
features were rescaled.

\subsection{D: Intrinsic dimensionality}\label{sec:dim}
We project D3 onto its first 2, 3, 4, 5 and 7 principal components and re-tune
both methods in each subspace (Figure~\ref{fig:abl}, Table~\ref{tab:dim}). The
result is a clean crossover.

\begin{table}[h]\centering\small
\caption{Ablation D: performance versus dimensionality on D3. ``viable
$\varepsilon$'' is the fraction of a 40-point $\varepsilon$ grid producing
$\geq2$ clusters with $<50\%$ noise.}
\label{tab:dim}
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{PCA dims} & \textbf{DBSCAN ARI} & \textbf{K-Means++ ARI} &
\textbf{viable $\varepsilon$} & \textbf{winner} \\
\midrule
2 & \textbf{0.575} & 0.468 & 0.125 & DBSCAN \\
3 & \textbf{0.618} & 0.466 & 0.675 & DBSCAN \\
4 & 0.453 & \textbf{0.512} & 0.575 & K-Means++ \\
5 & 0.399 & \textbf{0.520} & 0.675 & K-Means++ \\
7 & 0.455 & \textbf{0.731} & 0.650 & K-Means++ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

At two and three dimensions DBSCAN is clearly better (0.575 and 0.618 against
0.468 and 0.466). Between three and four dimensions the ranking reverses, and by
seven dimensions K-Means++ leads by 0.276 ARI --- a margin larger than the entire
range of DBSCAN's performance across the sweep. DBSCAN's ARI peaks at three
dimensions and then declines, while K-Means++ improves monotonically from four
dimensions onward.

This is the curse of dimensionality acting on a density estimator. As $d$ grows,
pairwise distances concentrate: the ratio of the nearest to the farthest
neighbour distance tends to one, so the $\varepsilon$-ball that separates
``dense'' from ``sparse'' becomes progressively less discriminative. K-Means is
comparatively insensitive because it compares each point only to $k$ centroids
rather than relying on local neighbourhood counts, and because the additional
principal components carry class-discriminative variance that its objective can
exploit.

This ablation also explains the main table. D1 and D2 are two-dimensional, where
density methods are strongest; D3 is seven-dimensional, where they are weakest.
The apparent contradiction between ``DBSCAN wins on spatial data'' and ``K-Means++
wins on Ecoli'' is not a contradiction at all --- it is a single monotone
relationship with intrinsic dimensionality, observed at two points.

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figs/f7_ablations.png}
\caption{Ablations C and D. Left: ARI versus PCA dimensionality, showing the
crossover between three and four dimensions. Centre: the usable $\varepsilon$
region. Right: effect of standardisation, which helps K-Means++ substantially and
does not help DBSCAN.}
\label{fig:abl}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Runtime}
At the selected operating points (Figure~\ref{fig:rt}), K-Means is fastest on
every dataset, DBSCAN costs roughly $3$--$6\times$ more, and HDBSCAN is the
outlier: 3.33\,s on D1 against 0.04\,s for K-Means, an $87\times$ gap. At
$n\leq$ 23{,}412 none of these differences is operationally meaningful --- all
four methods finish in seconds --- so runtime should not drive the choice at this
scale. The asymptotics do differ, however: K-Means is $O(nkdi)$ and DBSCAN
$O(n\log n)$ with a spatial index, so the ordering would change at much larger
$n$ with small $k$.

\begin{figure}[h]\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.55\textwidth]{figs/f9_runtime.png}
\caption{Runtime, log scale. HDBSCAN's hierarchy construction dominates on D1.}
\label{fig:rt}
\end{figure}

% ============================================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Successful cases}
DBSCAN on D1 at $\varepsilon=274$\,km is the clearest success in the study: 68
clusters that follow the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, the East Pacific Rise and the
circum-Pacific subduction zones, with 2\% of events labelled noise --- a figure
consistent with the expected rate of intraplate seismicity. No value of $k$ would
let K-Means produce this, because the clusters are one-dimensional curves
embedded in two dimensions.

DBSCAN on D2 at the k-distance knee separates the airports from the Manhattan
core, which is the operationally important distinction for a fleet operator, and
does so with 4\% noise.

K-Means++ on D3 is the third success, and it is a success for the opposite
reason: the Ecoli classes are compact blobs in a seven-dimensional space with no
meaningful density gaps between them, which is the regime K-Means was designed
for.

\subsection{Failure cases}
\textbf{K-Means on D1} is a textbook failure: three convex wedges imposed on a
curvilinear structure, with high silhouette and zero scientific content. The
failure is not of tuning but of representational capacity.

\textbf{HDBSCAN on D3} fails by over-discarding: 37\% of proteins labelled noise,
with the rare localisation classes among them, giving the worst ARI in the study
while simultaneously achieving the best silhouette. A method that improves its
score by deleting the hard cases is dangerous precisely when the hard cases
matter.

\textbf{Silhouette-driven selection on D2} fails by merging: the optimum collapses
Manhattan into one cluster, which is a correct statement about the geometry (the
density really is continuous there) and a useless one for the application.

\subsection{Key factors affecting performance}
Ordered by the magnitude of their effect in our experiments:
\textbf{(i) intrinsic dimensionality} --- 0.28 ARI swing on D3 and a reversal of
the method ranking (Section~\ref{sec:dim});
\textbf{(ii) initialisation} --- 0.24 ARI on D3 at fixed $k$ (Section~\ref{sec:init});
\textbf{(iii) feature scaling} --- 0.23 ARI for K-Means++ (Section~\ref{sec:scaling});
\textbf{(iv) $\varepsilon$} --- moves cluster count over two orders of magnitude
(Figure~\ref{fig:dbgrid});
\textbf{(v) cluster convexity} --- decides the outcome qualitatively on D1 but
cannot be read off any single number;
\textbf{(vi) \texttt{minPts}} --- a secondary control;
\textbf{(vii) runtime} --- irrelevant at this scale.

\subsection{Practical guidance}\label{sec:guidance}
\begin{enumerate}\itemsep1pt
\item \textbf{Decide by geometry, not by score.} Plot the data first. If the
structure is curvilinear or branching, no amount of $k$-tuning will help; if it
is blob-like in more than four dimensions, no amount of $\varepsilon$-tuning
will.
\item \textbf{Never select parameters by unconstrained silhouette on non-convex
data.} It reliably prefers fewer, rounder clusters and a larger discard rate.
Constrain the search by what the application needs first, then optimise inside
that region.
\item \textbf{Report the noise fraction alongside every internal index.} A
silhouette computed after discarding 37\% of the data is not comparable to one
computed after discarding none.
\item \textbf{Always use k-means++ seeding.} It is free and, on D3, worth 0.24
ARI at identical $k$.
\item \textbf{Prefer $\varepsilon$ to $k$ when the units are meaningful.}
``Hotspots are neighbourhoods within 700\,m'' is a statement a domain expert can
check; ``there are six hotspots'' is not.
\item \textbf{Project geographic coordinates.} Raw degrees give an anisotropic
distance and changed the D2 cluster count from 3 to 6.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Limitations}
Our spatial evaluation rests on internal indices whose reliability this very
paper questions; the D3 result gives a reason to distrust them but not a
replacement. Ground truth for D1 could in principle be constructed from published
plate-boundary geometries, which we did not attempt. All datasets are at most
seven-dimensional and at most 23{,}412 points, so the runtime comparison probes
only a narrow regime. We compare four methods from two families; spectral
clustering, Gaussian mixtures and OPTICS would extend the picture. Finally, the
D3 oracle protocol reports each method's best achievable ARI, which is optimistic
relative to what an analyst without labels could obtain.

% ============================================================
\section{Conclusion}

We compared K-Means, K-Means++, DBSCAN and HDBSCAN on two spatial hotspot-detection
tasks and one labelled biological benchmark. The headline empirical result is
that the standard tool for model selection in the unlabelled setting --- the
silhouette coefficient --- ranks the four methods in nearly the reverse of their
true order when ground truth is available (rank correlation $-0.80$), and on the
spatial data prefers operating points that are demonstrably useless for the
application: 29\% of earthquakes discarded rather than 2\%, three merged demand
zones rather than eleven usable ones. The strongest single piece of evidence is
Figure~\ref{fig:op}, where the setting that recovers the tectonic plate
boundaries almost exactly scores a silhouette of $-0.03$.

Underlying the method comparison is a single monotone factor. A dimensionality
ablation on the labelled data shows DBSCAN leading below four dimensions and
K-Means++ leading above it, which reconciles the apparently contradictory results
across our three datasets: the two-dimensional spatial problems and the
seven-dimensional biological one sit on opposite sides of one crossover.

Two directions follow. First, constructing approximate ground truth for the
spatial datasets from published plate-boundary or administrative geometries would
let the metric-disagreement result be tested directly where it matters most.
Second, the failure modes we document --- non-convexity for centroids, density
variation and high dimensionality for density estimators --- are exactly what
spectral clustering and deep embedded clustering are designed to address, and a
comparison extended in that direction is the natural next step.

